Original commit body from 17d48a8774c149dce3bd5cb44c074d40144d7bdf:

Addresses INV-tanom and WI-fuvaj (Go half). go.py stamped
call_construct="method" on every selector call, so http.Get(u) and c.Get(u)
were byte-identical in (name, module slot, construct). base.py defines
"method" as a call on a receiver, and ADR-0059 files a call on the module
itself as a function.

On 2026-09-09 this was priced at zero: its only consumer, io-boundary's
no-module gate, never sees a package-qualified call. 8.1.0's
unknown_receiver_scope caveat then used call_construct == "method" as its
denominator, so every fmt.Println counted as a method call site.

The unresolved-selector emit now stamps "function" when the operand is an
import alias, the same test that already fills the module slot. Module slots
are unchanged. A local that shadows its import is still treated as the
package; that is pinned by a strict xfail, because var_types is per function
and consulting it would also mark the url.Parse that defines the local as a
method call.

A/B on alertmanager and gocryptfs (dev pinned worktree vs this branch,
generic taint claims plus six boundary claims): no verdict, caveat kind or
evidence count moves. The caveat's denominator goes 6,183 -> 3,557
(alertmanager, 18.5% -> 32.1% untyped) and 3,345 -> 1,779 (gocryptfs,
8.5% -> 16.0%). go.yaml has no method-kind row on a package-path module, so
method_starved_modules cannot newly fire.

Not fixed: Java qualified statics are still stamped "method". Restamping
them waits on java.yaml declaring its statics function-kind; the
unknown_receiver_scope docstring now says so.

